  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM MST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nPerfect fall weather today with mainly clear skies and near- \nnormal temps underneath an amplified upper ridge. The ridge is \nnudged east by an advancing shortwave that comes across the region\nFriday night. Increasing moisture is expected for Friday with \nmid-high clouds overspreading the region. \n\nPWs climb to about 200% of normal Fri evening with diffluent flow\noverhead, meaning scattered to widespread showers. Far west TX is\nmost favored to see rain Fri evening with a wave of showers \nmodeled to move from SW to NE. Rain amounts of about 0.5\" are \nforecast E of the US 54 corridor with up to 1\" in isolated spots. \nPeriods of moderate rain rates are likely for eastern areas. A few\nembedded t-storms are possible, but instability will be meager \n(CAPE of <500 J/kg). Lower rain amounts are expected to the north \nand west. \n\nThat shortwave exits to the east on Sat but with a negatively \ntilted trough on its heels. Precip chances will be focused over \nnorthern NM, clipping area mtns with a low chance of rain/snow mix\nthrough Sat. Snow accum will be minimal as snow levels struggle \nto get below 9kft. A decent pressure gradient develops on the \nunderbelly of the northern shortwave on Sat behind the Pacific \nfront, resulting in breezy W winds. Gusts to 30-35 mph are \nexpected Sat afternoon with cooler temps.\n\nA shortwave ridge moves over the region early next week, keeping \nus dry with lighter winds. The next Pacific storm to watch passes \nby the Four Corners Tue/Wed, producing some chance of rain for us \ndepending on its track. The more northerly track shown by the 12z \nGFS keeps us mostly dry with some breezy SW winds. Otherwise, \ntypical fall weather is forecast into next week with temps running\na few degrees below normal.",
